00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:14.500
So think [INAUDIBLE] Turner
or Clark Construction.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.140
We do those bodies of work.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:23.290
We do all of this
effectively with technology.

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:26.260
And I'm going to talk a
little bit about partnership

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:30.010
and effective use of technology
at the end of this discussion.

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:33.640
But we do this on behalf of you.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:34.900
Many of you in this space.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:37.870
If you're a US citizen or a
visitor to the United States,

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:39.880
you're part of who
we do this for.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:41.960
So who are we?

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:45.490
We're the United States General
Services Administration.

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:48.490
And we do all of this on behalf
of the citizens and visitors

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:51.580
of the United States, as
well as the federal agencies

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:55.220
that support operations
in the federal space.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:57.940
So that's a pretty
picture of our place.

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:03.310
That's one of the many buildings
that we manage and control,

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:05.180
along with the Pentagon
and the White House,

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.040
and others like that.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.220
So all of this disparate
business mission,

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:12.010
we're effectively the back
office of the United States.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:16.690
We look after land, and
buildings, and physical needs.

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:19.060
Our workers are our
property managers,

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.670
they're purchasing agents,
they're policy writers.

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:25.420
We write policy for the
entire federal government.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:27.490
They're technologists,
we provide technology

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:29.470
for the federal government.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:32.560
And we do this on behalf
of our federal partners.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.190
So that our federal partners can
focus on their primary mission

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:39.370
areas, which are
protecting our borders,

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:43.720
and accelerating the mission
to Mars, or curing cancer.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:47.320
We do this kind of mundane
work, so that they can focus

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:49.450
on their primary mission areas.

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:52.090
Because we've been doing
that for a long time,

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:54.820
we've become a proving
ground, a test ground,

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:57.760
for new and enabling
technologies.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.550
So that what we can
try before we buy,

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:03.310
and try before we use
in GSA, we can then

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:07.780
scale across the
rest of government.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:11.650
So talking about that
digital transformation role.

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:15.310
Because we've used technology
as an enabler for a fair amount

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:20.140
of time, we've been able to take
what we've learned internally

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:23.440
and scale that through our
federal acquisition service,

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:26.530
we're the purchasing agents
of the federal government.

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:30.400
Or through internet of things
with our building automation

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.450
technologies, and we'll scale
that through government.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:39.960
Taking our IoT learnings
and moved on from there.

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:41.590
Let's go to the next one.

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:43.510
So how do we do this?

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:47.030
In this role, we
do a few things.

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:49.240
One, we try new technologies.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:52.870
We tracked world class
talent into our organization.

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:54.850
And we did this for
a couple of reasons.

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:56.510
We're steward of
taxpayer dollars.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:58.690
We're spending your money.

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:01.090
A shareholder, when they make
an investment in a company,

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:04.750
they're going into that knowing
that they can potentially

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:05.530
lose that money.

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:07.900
You, citizens, you're not
making that investment

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:12.400
or obligated to do well for you.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.970
That's a significant influence
on how we do our technology

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:18.078
and why we do our technology.

00:04:18.078 --> 00:04:20.620
Because of that, because we're
dealing with taxpayer dollars,

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.480
we share everything.

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:24.850
We share our code, we
share our playbooks,

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:27.370
we share our configurations,
we share talent,

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:29.110
we share our infrastructure.

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:32.650
Because we're doing
this with your dollars.

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:36.820
In addition to being
able to share everything,

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:38.740
the ideation that
we do, the builds

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:42.530
of our minimally viable
products, how we deliver,

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:45.940
how we determine what works
and what doesn't work.

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:49.120
We get some really good
outcomes by doing that,

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.120
by rapidly iterating
to things that work,

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:55.480
and rapidly pivoting away
from things that don't.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:57.280
And that allows us to
be sort of a training

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:59.890
center for effective
use of technology,

00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:02.620
not only in the federal
government, but elsewhere.

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:07.420
Some examples are people
from my team, GSA IT,

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:09.160
are now the chief
technology officer

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:11.960
for Department of
Homeland Security,

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:15.730
or run the federal
practice at Salesforce,

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:19.240
or run the federal
practice at Box.

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:20.782
Because they learn
what they can do,

00:05:20.782 --> 00:05:22.990
what the art of the possible
is in federal government

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:26.060
here and then export
that out elsewhere.

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:28.510
Some of the results of this
mindset, this innovation

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:30.940
mindset, are up on the screen.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:33.460
We've been able to rationalize
our applications down

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:35.380
to about 20%.

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:38.530
The way we deliver,
we've been able to save

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:43.270
17.8% of our costs,
while also increasing

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:46.720
employee satisfaction with
what we do and how we do it.

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:49.110
That's over $100 million
recurring annually

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:50.860
that we've been able
to save while driving

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:53.830
higher, more effective
adoption of technologies.

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:56.740
And my customer
satisfaction rate is 91%.

00:05:56.740 --> 00:06:01.520
Let that sink in for a
minute, 91% in the IT space.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:04.750
I'm very proud of that.

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:07.580
So to the comms
team, this is where

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:09.580
I'm going to kind of go
off script a little bit.

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:12.520
So you're free to go get a
cup of coffee or something

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:13.090
like that.

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:14.296
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:17.590
So Tarik, at the beginning,
talked about partnership.

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:19.840
And that's where I want
to end this conversation,

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:22.240
because partnership
is key and parcel

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:24.010
to everything that we do.

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:29.110
When we started our
transformation play in 2011,

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:32.410
there were zero agencies in the
federal government that were

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:35.230
using cloud-based email, zero.

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:38.470
And so when we said,
we need to find a lever

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:41.890
to drive transformation
into the federal government,

00:06:41.890 --> 00:06:44.140
we had to be very intentional
about what we did.

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:47.198
Because the technology piece,
frankly, is the easy part.

00:06:47.198 --> 00:06:49.240
The hard part's organizational
change management,

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:53.980
driving adoption of new
technologies into a new space.

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:56.020
And so we had to be very
intentional about what

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:57.220
partner we chose.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:06:59.480
We happened to choose Google.

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:05.020
So we partnered with Google
to flip from 17 mail systems

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:07.710
into a cloud-based Gmail system.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:09.670
Saw some really
good outcomes there.

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:12.080
And what that did is
allowed me to do two things.

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:15.430
One, in my organization to
say, look, if you transform,

00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:17.530
it's not the end of
your business world.

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:22.000
Your business world will be able
to continue to grow and thrive.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.790
And that provided a
springboard to the next step.

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:27.530
And we said, OK, what's
the next logical target?

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:30.970
We had employees storing
data on their laptops,

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:34.510
and on their phones, and in any
space where they could possibly

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:35.410
find it.

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:37.750
We said, well, let's put a
controlled environment there

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:39.042
and move that out to the cloud.

00:07:39.042 --> 00:07:42.580
So we moved to Google Drive and
had all 16,000 of our employees

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:44.440
move their data out
to Google Drive.

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:45.860
Really great outcomes.

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:48.250
People saw they could
still access their stuff.

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:50.740
They could get to
it from anywhere.

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:55.360
And then they saw
those great outcomes.

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:57.130
And then we moved
to Google Docs,

00:07:57.130 --> 00:08:00.370
and then we moved to
Google's scripting

00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:02.300
to allow us to do
business process.

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:07.570
And then we moved on to Google
Cloud, amongst other clouds.

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:09.330
And so that lever,
that's the thing

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:10.770
I want to leave everybody with--

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:12.360
when you're choosing
your partners,

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:14.700
you're transformation agents
in your organizations,

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:16.350
choose very carefully.

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:19.590
Because they can be the one
thin line, the discriminator,

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:21.690
between success and failure.

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:24.540
In that regard, we're super
proud that we used Google

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:28.190
as that primary transformation
agent from the very beginning.

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:28.690
That's it.

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:29.940
Thank you very much.

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:34.490
